A Complete \u1055?\u1072?\u1090?\u1090?\u1077?\u1088?\u1085? With Dynamics \pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b \u1055?\u1086?\u1083?\u1085?\u1099?\u1081? \u1087?\u1072?\u1090?\u1090?\u1077?\u1088?\u1085? \u1089? \u1076?\u1080?\u1085?\u1072?\u1084?\u1080?\u1082?\u1086?\u1081?}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Here is a full three-row pattern with velocity applied throughout. The hi-hat has accents on the beats. The snare has loud main hits with ghost notes woven between. The kick hits at full velocity on its active steps. Together, they form a pattern with shape and weight.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf5\i [\u1044?\u1080?\u1072?\u1075?\u1088?\u1072?\u1084?\u1084?\u1072? \u8212? \u1089?\u1084?. HTML-\u1074?\u1077?\u1088?\u1089?\u1080?\u1102? \u1076?\u1083?\u1103? \u1080?\u1083?\u1083?\u1102?\u1089?\u1090?\u1088?\u1072?\u1094?\u1080?\u1080?]\par
\u1055?\u1086?\u1083?\u1085?\u1099?\u1081? \u1087?\u1072?\u1090?\u1090?\u1077?\u1088?\u1085? \u1089? \u1076?\u1080?\u1085?\u1072?\u1084?\u1080?\u1082?\u1086?\u1081? \u8212? \u1087?\u1088?\u1086?\u1079?\u1088?\u1072?\u1095?\u1085?\u1086?\u1089?\u1090?\u1100? \u1086?\u1090?\u1086?\u1073?\u1088?\u1072?\u1078?\u1072?\u1077?\u1090? \u1074?\u1077?\u1083?\u1086?\u1089?\u1080?\u1090?\u1080? \pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Compare this to a flat pattern where every hit is the same volume. The notes are identical \u8212? the feel is completely different.\par
Superstition \pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b \u1042?\u1086?\u1089?\u1089?\u1086?\u1079?\u1076?\u1072?\u1105?\u1084? \u171?Superstition\u187? \u8212? \u1057?\u1090?\u1080?\u1074?\u1080? \u1059?\u1072?\u1085?\u1076?\u1077?\u1088?}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 One of the most dynamic drum grooves ever recorded. The drum part on "Superstition" (1972) is a masterclass in velocity control \u8212? every hit has a specific dynamic purpose. The ghost notes on the snare weave between the kick pattern, creating an interlocking groove that defines the song.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b \u1055?\u1072?\u1090?\u1090?\u1077?\u1088?\u1085?}\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Hi-Hat: sixteenth notes (all 16 steps), accented on beats 1, 5, 9, 13\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Snare: main hits on steps 5, 13 (high velocity) + ghost notes on steps 3, 7, 10, 15 (very low velocity)\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 \bullet  Kick: steps 1, 7, 9, 11, 13 (syncopated funk pattern)\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs20\cf5\i [\u1044?\u1080?\u1072?\u1075?\u1088?\u1072?\u1084?\u1084?\u1072? \u8212? \u1089?\u1084?. HTML-\u1074?\u1077?\u1088?\u1089?\u1080?\u1102? \u1076?\u1083?\u1103? \u1080?\u1083?\u1083?\u1102?\u1089?\u1090?\u1088?\u1072?\u1094?\u1080?\u1080?]\par
\u1041?\u1072?\u1088?\u1072?\u1073?\u1072?\u1085?\u1085?\u1099?\u1081? \u1087?\u1072?\u1090?\u1090?\u1077?\u1088?\u1085? \u171?Superstition\u187? \u8212? \u1090?\u1077?\u1087?\u1083?\u1086?\u1074?\u1072?\u1103? \u1082?\u1072?\u1088?\u1090?\u1072? \u1074?\u1077?\u1083?\u1086?\u1089?\u1080?\u1090?\u1080? \pard\sa200\f0\fs22 The ghost notes on the snare (steps 3, 7, 10, 15) weave between the kick pattern, creating an interlocking groove. Notice how the kick on step 7 lines up with a snare ghost on step 7 \u8212? both happening simultaneously, one loud and one barely there. This layering is funk: every sound has a specific dynamic purpose.\par
How to Apply Velocity \pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b \u1050?\u1072?\u1082? \u1087?\u1088?\u1080?\u1084?\u1077?\u1085?\u1103?\u1090?\u1100? \u1074?\u1077?\u1083?\u1086?\u1089?\u1080?\u1090?\u1080? \u1074? GarageBand}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Step 1: \u1053?\u1072?\u1078?\u1072?\u1090?\u1080?\u1077? the Velocity button below the grid. The view changes to show velocity bars for each active step.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Step 2: Drag UP on a step to increase velocity (louder). Drag DOWN to decrease (softer).\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Step 3: Drag across multiple steps to set a gradient \u8212? useful for creating a swell or a fade.\par
\pard\sb300\sa100{\f0\fs24\b \u1055?\u1088?\u1072?\u1082?\u1090?\u1080?\u1095?\u1077?\u1089?\u1082?\u1080?\u1081? \u1087?\u1086?\u1076?\u1093?\u1086?\u1076?}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Start with all steps at medium velocity (around 80\u8211?90). Then raise the important beats \u8212? the accents \u8212? to full velocity (120\u8211?127). Finally, lower the in-between hits and ghost notes to 20\u8211?40. This three-layer approach (loud, medium, soft) gives you immediate dynamic shape without overthinking individual values.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 You don't need mathematical precision. The ear responds to relative differences. As long as accents are noticeably louder and ghost notes are noticeably softer, the groove works.\par
Exploration \pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b \u1069?\u1082?\u1089?\u1087?\u1077?\u1088?\u1080?\u1084?\u1077?\u1085?\u1090?\u1099?}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 Take any beat you built in the previous tutorials and apply what you've learned here:\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 1.  Accent the hi-hats. Set steps 1, 5, 9, and 13 to high velocity. Set the remaining steps to low velocity. Notice how the groove immediately develops a pulse, even though no notes were added or removed.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 2.  Add ghost notes to the snare. Place 2\u8211?3 snare hits at very low velocity between the main backbeats. Does the pattern feel more alive? More complex?\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 3.  Weight the downbeat. Make the kick on step 1 louder than the kick on step 9. This gives the pattern a sense of "downbeat weight" \u8212? a clear beginning to each measure that the listener can anchor to.\par
\pard\li720\fi-360\sa100\f0\fs22 4.  Experiment with the hi-hat gradient. Instead of just two levels (loud/soft), try making steps 1 and 9 loudest, steps 5 and 13 slightly less, and everything else soft. This creates a four-bar-feeling within a single measure.\par
Closing \pard\sb400\sa150{\f0\fs28\b \u1047?\u1072?\u1082?\u1083?\u1102?\u1095?\u1077?\u1085?\u1080?\u1077?}\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 \u1042?\u1077?\u1083?\u1086?\u1089?\u1080?\u1090?\u1080? \u8212? \u1101?\u1090?\u1086? \u1090?\u1086?, \u1095?\u1090?\u1086? \u1086?\u1090?\u1076?\u1077?\u1083?\u1103?\u1077?\u1090? \u1087?\u1072?\u1090?\u1090?\u1077?\u1088?\u1085? \u1086?\u1090? \u1080?\u1089?\u1087?\u1086?\u1083?\u1085?\u1077?\u1085?\u1080?\u1103?. \u1058?\u1072? \u1078?\u1077? \u1087?\u1086?\u1089?\u1083?\u1077?\u1076?\u1086?\u1074?\u1072?\u1090?\u1077?\u1083?\u1100?\u1085?\u1086?\u1089?\u1090?\u1100? \u1085?\u1086?\u1090?, \u1086?\u1092?\u1086?\u1088?\u1084?\u1083?\u1077?\u1085?\u1085?\u1072?\u1103? \u1076?\u1080?\u1085?\u1072?\u1084?\u1080?\u1082?\u1086?\u1081?, \u1089?\u1090?\u1072?\u1085?\u1086?\u1074?\u1080?\u1090?\u1089?\u1103? \u1084?\u1091?\u1079?\u1099?\u1082?\u1086?\u1081?, \u1082?\u1086?\u1090?\u1086?\u1088?\u1072?\u1103? \u1076?\u1099?\u1096?\u1080?\u1090?. \u1055?\u1083?\u1086?\u1089?\u1082?\u1072?\u1103? \u1089?\u1077?\u1090?\u1082?\u1072? \u8212? \u1101?\u1090?\u1086? \u1095?\u1077?\u1088?\u1090?\u1105?\u1078?. \u1057?\u1077?\u1090?\u1082?\u1072? \u1089? \u1074?\u1077?\u1083?\u1086?\u1089?\u1080?\u1090?\u1080? \u8212? \u1101?\u1090?\u1086? \u1075?\u1088?\u1091?\u1074?.\par
\pard\sa200\f0\fs22 In the next tutorial, we'll look at how changing the number of steps and the playback rate lets you build patterns that feel like they move in different subdivisions \u8212? triplets, half-time, and beyond.\par
